TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found 2026-08-31 by the coordinator while running the B067 class-position
scope campaign. Confirmed by minimal red demonstration, not inferred.

## Defect

`scripts/probe_enum_corpus.py` `build_source()` concatenates every name in a
batch (default 200) into ONE AngelScript compilation unit, one declaration per
line, and attributes diagnostics back to names by line number. `run_batch`
records a name as ACCEPTED when no message was attributed to its line.

A **parse** error anywhere in that unit aborts compilation before semantic
analysis, so no line gets a diagnostic — and every other name in the batch is
then recorded ACCEPTED. The failure is silent: the tool reports success and
emits a full corpus.

## Red demonstration (verbatim, 2026-08-31)

Isolated (`batch=1`):

    REJECT  CMwNod::ZZZNoSuchName   Identifier 'ZZZNoSuchName' is not a data type in namespace 'CMwNod' or parent
    REJECT  int::ZZZNoSuchName      Expected identifier

The same two names in ONE batch:

    ACCEPT  CMwNod::ZZZNoSuchName
    REJECT  int::ZZZNoSuchName      Expected identifier

`CMwNod::ZZZNoSuchName` flips REJECT -> ACCEPT purely by being batched with a
name that fails to parse. `int::` is a parse error because `int` is a keyword.

## Why this is the dangerous direction

The tool's own docstring says a REJECT is evidence and an ACCEPT may be an
artefact. This defect manufactures exactly the untrustworthy verdict: it turns
solid REJECTs into weak ACCEPTs, and ACCEPT is the direction the repo already
knows not to lean on. It is also self-consistent — the corpus looks complete
and internally sensible.

This first bit me directly: my initial B067 control run reported that
`ZZZNoSuchClass::ZZZNoSuchName` (a name I invented) was ACCEPTED, along with
`Audio`/`Net`/`Meta`/`GameData`, which contradicted B056's established rule.
Re-running with `batch=1` gave the correct answers for all of them. Had I not
run a negative control, I would have recorded a fabricated name as accepted by
the game and "disproved" B056.

## UNVERIFIED and important: are the shipped corpora affected?

`docs/probes/b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv` (1905 rows, **742 ACCEPTED**)
and `b053-bare-enum-values.tsv` were generated by this tool in batches of 200,
i.e. ~10 batches. If any batch contained a name that failed to PARSE, every
later-attributed name in that batch is wrongly ACCEPTED.

**I could not check this.** The re-verification run started and the game exited
mid-run (`RemoteBuild is not alive`), so the sample never completed. Do not
record the corpora as sound on the strength of this ticket — the question is
open, and it is the main reason this is not merely a tooling nicety. B052,
B056 and B057 all cite those corpora.

## Work

1. **Fix the attribution.** Options, in preference order:
   - Submit one name per compilation unit. Simple and obviously correct;
     cost is one round trip per name (measured: fine for hundreds of names).
   - Keep batching for speed but DETECT the abort: if the unit produced a
     parse-class diagnostic, or produced fewer attributed lines than expected,
     re-run that batch name-by-name instead of emitting ACCEPTED.
   - Never record ACCEPTED by absence-of-message alone. Absence must be
     distinguishable from "analysis never ran".
2. **Re-verify the shipped corpora** once the game is up: re-probe the ACCEPTED
   rows in isolation and diff. Report the flip count. If non-zero, regenerate
   and tell B056/B057 what moved.
3. Add a regression test using the demonstration above: a batch containing a
   known parse-error name must not report its companions as ACCEPTED.

## Test plan

- the two-name red demo above goes green (companion keeps its REJECT)
- a batch with NO parse error still returns identical verdicts to isolated runs
  (so the fix does not change good-path answers)
- a name that is a genuine semantic reject is still REJECTED in a batch
- a name that the game genuinely accepts is still ACCEPTED
- the corpus regeneration path (`--b052` / `--b053`) exercises the fix

